# Render coherence/assemble-rR's brief (R >= 3): the workflow's assemble prompt + ROUND-R NOTES.  Usage: python3 mkasm.py R <base sha256 of docs/ARCHITECTURE.md before this round>
import json, os, subprocess, sys
SP = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
R = int(sys.argv[1]); BASE = sys.argv[2]; P = R - 1
D = f'{SP}/rr{R}'; os.makedirs(D, exist_ok=True)
json.dump({'round': R}, open(f'{D}/asm-args.json', 'w'))
subprocess.run(['node', f'{SP}/prompts.mjs', 'assemble', f'{D}/asm-args.json', f'{D}/assemble-r{R}.raw.txt'], check=True)
note = f"""

ROUND-{R} NOTES (orchestrator, 2026-10-01): (1) The block files are exactly docs/validation/coherence/repair-r{R}-<unit>.md, one per unit (18: model, kernel, pipeline, determinism, rendering, scope, V01–V12); everything under docs/validation/coherence/work/ and the bench files are the repair seats' working material, never blocks. Before applying, record the sha256 of every live file you may write (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/ARCHITECTURE-RATIONALE.md, docs/volumes/V*.md, docs/volumes/INDEX.md) in your report; after, the new sha256. (2) "=== RULING REQUEST <id> ===" blocks are never applied; none is expected this round (every repair-r{R} result reports 0) — if one appears, skip it and list it. (3) apply-blocks.py works on one document: for the volume units, split each file's "=== FILE docs/volumes/<name>.md ===" sections and run the applier with --doc on that volume; blocks for the base go through it on docs/ARCHITECTURE.md with the rationale file. A repair file may carry blocks for more than one file: route each block to the file its nearest FILE line names (base when none). (4) A double REPLACE of one heading (two units repairing one subsection): the merge base for git merge-file is the subsection as it stands in the live file before this round — the text assemble-r{P} installed (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md sha256 {BASE[:16]}…). Report every conflict and keep the first block's text where the merge conflicts, listing the lost hunk for the verifiers. (5) A block whose heading is missing: before skipping, check whether an earlier block of this round renamed or renumbered it; where one block clearly targets a heading another block of this round just retitled, apply it to the retitled heading and report it; otherwise skip and report. (6) The document checks stay PROVISIONAL in this checkout (the reference corpus is incomplete): run them as rounds 0–{P} did (untouched, new, combined), review every hit, add allow rows only for reviewed false positives, mark the counts provisional. (7) Then the bundle (python3 tools/bundle_architecture.py, then --check). (8) Report per unit: blocks found, applied, skipped (with reason), and the words/KB before and after. (9) ROUND-{R} SPECIFICS. (a) Some repair files carry ASSEMBLE NOTES or swap instructions in their headers (J-ROWS and RATIONALE-A rows restated, a row swapped into an older row's place): apply each mechanically as written and list it. (b) The repairs were written against docs/validation/coherence/adjudication-r{R}.md's Part H, whose END lines put one fix's two ends in two units' files: apply every file before you run the reference check, since a cross-unit citation resolves only once both ends are in. (c) After applying, count design-text citations of review ids (`R2-nn`, `R3-nn`, adjudication or verify ids) in the live base and volumes outside the decision and request records, and report the count and sites (adjudication-r{R}'s R3-02 says none; do not edit them yourself — the verifiers raise them). (d) Assemble only after all eighteen repair-r{R} files have LANDED (their results in docs/validation/coherence/results/coherence_repair-<unit>-r{R}.json)."""
raw = open(f'{D}/assemble-r{R}.raw.txt').read().rstrip('\n') + note
open(f'{D}/assemble-r{R}.full.txt', 'w').write(raw)
subprocess.run(['python3', f'{SP}/finalize.py', f'coherence/assemble-r{R}', f'{D}/assemble-r{R}.full.txt'], check=True)
